<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cantar de mio Cid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · Also known as Poema de mio Cid, Poem of the Cid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cantar de mio Cid (Poem of the Cid) is the oldest surviving epic poem in Castilian Spanish, telling of the exile and triumphs of Rodrigo Díaz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>. Composed around 1200, it survives in a single 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copied from a text signed by a certain Per Abbat. It recounts the Cid's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and the restoration of his honour, and is a founding work of Spanish literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid.jpg" alt="Cantar de mio Cid" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Per Abbat (copista) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cantar_de_mio_Cid_f._1r_(rep).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
